To assess various adaptation techniques for introducing new languages into a large, extensively trained language model, we removed 16 languages from the BigScience multitasking dataset and evaluated how well it could learn these additional languages using three different methods. Among the methods tested, backtranslation showed the most promising results, successfully achieving accurate translations for newly added languages within just a limited number of adaptations. Notably, a more sophisticated training approach that combined backtranslation with learning tasks for existing languages yielded exceptionally high performance levels, achieving mean scores of 42.5 and 43.6 for questions and tasks, respectively. Notably, this approach not only improved results but also promoted generalization across similar languages, such as Czech and Slovak, or German and Dutch. Additionally, incorporating a word-level denoising autoencoder for adaptation purposes failed to enhance translation quality but did improve results for questions. Crucially, the outstanding performance of the top-ranked model from BigScience underscores the potential of large models to translate unfamiliar languages when trained on a broad, diverse dataset of languages. The code and dataset used for these experiments are freely available. 

