\section{Introduction}

Recent breakthroughs in NLP have shown that large language models can achieve nearly perfect translation performance on existing language pairs when trained on extensive datasets. However, introducing new languages or expanding to unfamiliar ones poses a significant challenge for these models. To tackle this issue, we explored four different approaches to adaptation: (a) adding a new language to an existing training set, (b) removing an existing language from a training set, (c) combining both strategies to introduce a new language, and (d) using backtranslation to introduce a new language. Furthermore, we investigated the effect of unlabelled data on these adaptation methods by comparing the performance of the BigScience multitasking dataset with and without adaptation, as well as with and without unlabeled data.

\section{Methodology}

We used the BigScience dataset, which contains 16 languages and 46 tasks, to examine the impact of adaptation on a large language model. To simulate real-world scenarios, we devised three different methods for introducing new languages: (a) adding a new language to an existing training set, (b) removing an existing language from a training set, and (c) using backtranslation to introduce a new language. Moreover, we investigated the effect of unlabelled data on these adaptation methods by comparing the performance of the BigScience multitasking dataset with and without unlabeled data.

