A new statistical approach is developed for assessing sample sizes in clinical trials that study multiple subpopulations under one protocol. This approach uses Bayesian methods to share information across groups in a treatment trial. For each subpopulation, a sample size formula is adapted to account for the uncertainty and potential overlap in patient definitions between groups. By incorporating borrowing power, this approach allows for a unified analysis of data from all subgroups at various stages of the trial. Furthermore, our methodology is adapted to accommodate both fixed and random assignment of patients to groups. Notably, our approach is compatible with the FDA's guidance on sample size assessment for basket trials. Through simulations, the reliability and accuracy of our approach are verified and compared with alternative methods. An example from a real-world trial, as well as software code, are also provided to demonstrate the statistical analysis. Keywords: Basket trials, Sample size assessment, Basket trial sample size assessment, Subpopulations, Patient definitions, Uncertainty, Bayesian methods, Clinical trials. 

