\section{Introduction}
In recent years, "basket trials" have gained prominence as a flexible design for assessing the effectiveness and efficiency of targeted therapies in specific patient subgroups. Through this trial design, patients are not assigned to distinct groups based on the treatment they receive but rather are assigned to subgroups defined by the biological characteristic being targeted. As a result, patients receiving the same treatment may belong to different groups and vice versa. This chapter provides an overview of the statistical principles underlying basket trials and the methods used for assessing sample sizes in such studies. Furthermore, we present novel Bayesian methods for conducting basket trials that borrow information across different subpopulations and patient definitions. 

\section{Basket Trial Sample Size Assessment}
In recent years, the focus has been on assessing sample sizes in clinical trials that study multiple subgroups under one protocol. A comprehensive review of this topic was presented at the 30th International Conference on Statistical Sciences in 2019. This chapter presents a straightforward approach for evaluating sample sizes in clinical trials that utilize the Bayesian method and take into account diverse patient definitions and targeted treatments. Furthermore, we have developed a unified framework for analyzing data from all subgroups at various stages of the trial. 

