Accurate vertebrae localization and identification are crucial in various medical applications related to spine disorder diagnosis and surgical planning. However, certain patient demographics such as the elderly, significantly hinder optimal model performance. Our novel approach tackles this issue by first refining the spinal column for enhanced model reliability. Building on L2-UNet, a leading deep learning model for vertebrae alignment, we've developed an improved model, L2-UNet-Pro, which incorporates vertebrae center and identity constraints. An anatomically-constrained optimization method is then applied to generate and evaluate vertebrae position alternatives, based on the optimized findings of the L2-UNet-Pro model. Evaluation across 92 patients yielded outstanding results, with Center Distance metrics averaging 1.77mm, 2.08mm, 2.44mm for L2-UNet, L2-UNet-Pro, and Ground Truth, respectively. Our method has been successfully utilized in the spINE challenge held during MICCAI 2021 and the results demonstrate significant improvements in vertebrae localization accuracy. Code and model will be shared on GitHub. 
